{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_0f35fe42df7d52b40169bb414bbfb8819198810059dcb7e290", "created": 1773879627, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\n  \"title\": \"Cancer Post-Treatment Surveillance and Follow-up Testing (Guide, Schedules, and Modalities)\",\n  \"summary\": \"This community centers on the concept of SURVEILLANCE as a structured, risk-adapted post-treatment management approach designed to detect cancer RECURRENCE or NEW CANCER GROWTH early, typically initiated when there are NO SIGNS OF CANCER. The network links SURVEILLANCE to specific follow-up modalities (e.g., DIGITAL RECTAL EXAMINATION, FLEXIBLE SIGMOIDOSCOPY, ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND, COLONOSCOPY) and to a defined FOLLOW-UP SCHEDULE and FOLLOW-UP TESTING SCHEDULE that are referenced as being presented in a GUIDE. It also connects surveillance practices to particular disease contexts (e.g., STAGE 2 and STAGE 3 COLON CANCER; ANAL CANCER RECURRENCE; bladder cancer follow-up testing), and to staging/assessment concepts such as PELVIC MRI STAGING and evaluation of NEARBY LYMPH NODES, as well as procedure-specific considerations like TRANSANAL LOCAL EXCISION and its explicit omission of LYMPH NODE REMOVAL.\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Surveillance is the organizing hub: a long-term, structured strategy aimed at early detection of recurrence\",\n      \"explanation\": \"SURVEILLANCE is described as a management approach based on structured monitoring and observation rather than immediate active intervention, commonly used after a definitive management choice (e.g., after treatment is completed) to detect recurrence, progression, or new cancer growth as early as possible. It is characterized as ongoing, long-term follow-up with repeated assessments over multiple years. [Data: Entities (2167, 5808, 4518); Relationships (3726, 13152)]\\n\\nThe network explicitly defines the core clinical outcome being monitored: RECURRENCE (including phrased as “RECURRENCE (CANCER COMES BACK)”) is the key event surveillance is designed to identify after treatment/removal. This framing makes recurrence detection the primary functional purpose linking the other testing and schedule entities in this community. [Data: Entities (5808); Relationships (3726, 13152)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Follow-up cadence is formalized via schedules, with the Guide serving as a reference point for timing\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community specifies a FOLLOW-UP SCHEDULE for surveillance visits spanning multiple years: every 2–4 months for the first 2 years, every 3–6 months for the next 3 years, and then annually after 5 years. This establishes a structured cadence that can be used to standardize ongoing monitoring intensity over time. [Data: Entities (2331); Relationships (4059)]\\n\\nA separate FOLLOW-UP TESTING SCHEDULE is described as a structured timetable for surveillance tests that varies by cancer stage and risk, and the relationships indicate that this recommended schedule is shown in a GUIDE. This makes the GUIDE a central documentation node tying together recommended surveillance timing and patient-facing explanations of tests and procedures. [Data: Entities (4525, 117); Relationships (9581)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Surveillance operationalizes into concrete exam and imaging modalities, especially in rectal/anal follow-up contexts\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Within this network, SURVEILLANCE is linked to specific modalities and recommended intervals. DIGITAL RECTAL EXAMINATION (DRE) is recommended as part of surveillance (with defined frequency referenced as every 3–6 months for 5 years, and the entity noting initiation 8–12 weeks after chemoradiation). This illustrates surveillance as a repeatable, protocol-driven exam strategy rather than ad hoc monitoring. [Data: Entities (6173, 2167); Relationships (14142)]\\n\\nThe community also connects surveillance to endoscopic and imaging follow-up. FLEXIBLE SIGMOIDOSCOPY is described as occurring about every 6 months during surveillance after ESD (with an early first exam sometimes at 3 months), and ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND is recommended at intervals as part of imaging surveillance after ESD and as an option after transanal excision. Together these relationships show how surveillance is implemented through repeated direct visualization and imaging in defined patterns. [Data: Entities (2765, 2766); Relationships (5127, 5128)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Colon cancer surveillance is differentiated by stage, with a consistent set of monitoring tools (colonoscopy, physical exams, CEA tests, imaging)\",\n      \"explanation\": \"For STAGE 2 COLON CANCER and STAGE 3 COLON CANCER, the entities describe surveillance including colonoscopy plus additional monitoring such as regular PHYSICAL EXAMS, CEA BLOOD TESTS, and periodic CT scans to check for recurrence or progression/metastasis. This indicates a standardized multi-modal follow-up approach that escalates monitoring in higher-risk contexts (with stage 3 explicitly noted as having higher recurrence risk than earlier stages). [Data: Entities (4487, 4488, 2312, 4519); Relationships (9535, 9537)]\\n\\nThe relationships also explicitly connect COLONOSCOPY to SURVEILLANCE FOLLOW-UP TESTING with defined intervals for repeat testing, reinforcing colonoscopy as a core longitudinal measurement within post-treatment follow-up. [Data: Entities (4518); Relationships (9521)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Staging and nodal assessment concepts inform surveillance and management considerations (MRI and nearby lymph nodes)\",\n      \"explanation\": \"NEARBY LYMPH NODES are described as regional nodes close to the primary tumor site, commonly evaluated by imaging and/or removed and examined during surgery to determine spread, and central to N staging. In this community, PELVIC MRI STAGING is linked to NEARBY LYMPH NODES, with the relationship stating pelvic MRI staging can show involvement/extent in nearby lymph nodes. This indicates how surveillance and follow-up may rely on imaging-based nodal assessment to detect disease extent or recurrence patterns. [Data: Entities (821, 2755); Relationships (5092)]\\n\\nThe network further specifies that PELVIC MRI STAGING uses a RECTAL CANCER STAGING TECHNIQUE to evaluate disease extent and nodes, reinforcing the technical specificity of imaging in rectal cancer contexts and how staging techniques carry into follow-up usage (noted in the entity description). [Data: Entities (2755, 2787); Relationships (5116)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Procedure-specific tradeoffs are explicit: transanal local excision omits lymph node removal, affecting follow-up needs and technique choices\",\n      \"explanation\": \"TRANSANAL LOCAL EXCISION is described as a surgical approach for T1 tumors at the end of the rectum performed through the anus, removing all layers of the rectal wall along with SURROUNDING NORMAL RECTAL TISSUE to obtain a margin. The relationships emphasize a key procedural limitation: LYMPH NODE REMOVAL is explicitly not performed in transanal local excision. This omission can make surveillance and imaging/exam follow-up especially important for detecting regional disease that would otherwise be assessed surgically. [Data: Entities (2768, 2781, 2782); Relationships (5134, 5135)]\\n\\nThe community also links transanal local excision to advanced related techniques—TRANSANAL ENDOSCOPIC MICROSURGERY (TEM) and TRANSANAL MINIMALLY INVASIVE SURGERY (TAMIS)—described as options for tumors higher in the rectum. These relationships suggest a procedural ecosystem in which technique selection and follow-up planning are interconnected. [Data: Entities (2768, 2769, 2770); Relationships (5132, 5133)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Follow-up testing is framed as conditional on a ‘no signs of cancer’ state and aimed at identifying new growth early (including bladder cancer context)\",\n      \"explanation\": \"FOLLOW-UP TESTING is defined as ongoing surveillance testing performed after bladder cancer treatment when there are no current signs of cancer, carried out over many years to detect early evidence of new cancer growth or recurrence. The relationships specify that follow-up testing is started when there are NO SIGNS OF CANCER and helps find NEW CANCER GROWTH early, clarifying both the trigger condition and the operational goal. [Data: Entities (4511, 4512, 4513); Relationships (9516, 9517)]\\n\\nThis framing aligns with the broader community definition of surveillance as long-term, risk-adapted monitoring intended to detect relapse early while not being immediate therapeutic intervention. [Data: Entities (2167, 4518); Relationships (13152)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Documentation and patient education are embedded via the Guide and its survivorship content, but no legal/reputation signals are present in the provided data\",\n      \"explanation\": \"GUIDE is described as a referenced, patient-oriented informational resource providing structured educational content about diagnostic testing, interpretation of results, and recommended next steps, and it is explicitly referenced as the place where the recommended schedule for surveillance testing is presented. The relationships further connect the FOLLOW-UP TESTING SCHEDULE to the GUIDE, indicating the guide’s role as a coordinating reference for surveillance timing. [Data: Entities (117, 4525); Relationships (9581)]\\n\\nThe GUIDE also contains a SURVIVORSHIP CHAPTER referenced for more information on side effects (e.g., premature ovarian insufficiency or radiation-induced menopause), indicating that survivorship considerations are integrated with surveillance guidance. Additionally, the GUIDE includes information on ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA (ALL) per the relationship indicating a transit